{
  "$schema": "https://github.com/microsoft/azure-sql-skills-lab/spec/schemas/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "azuresql-db-faq",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": ["not-in-training-data", "corrects-a-wrong-assumption", "changes-too-fast-to-memorize"],
  "correction": "Asked whether the Azure SQL Database container supports some feature, an agent answers from general SQL Server and Azure knowledge and is confidently wrong in both directions at once. It promises managed service features the container cannot have, because those live around the engine rather than in it: automated backups, point in time restore, geo-replication, elastic pools, and any form of Azure portal management. And it denies engine features the container does have, or offers a SQL Server remedy that this engine refuses. The fix is not a longer list of facts, it is a way to sort the question. Every capability question falls into one of four buckets, and the bucket determines the answer: engine features are present, managed service features are absent by construction, SQL Server only features are absent because Azure SQL Database does not have them either, and a small set of things are incomplete because this is a Private Preview. The answers that need to be exact are exact and are not recallable. BACKUP DATABASE and RESTORE DATABASE return Msg 40510 in every session, so the local persistence answer is a Docker named volume rather than a backup file. USE against another database returns Msg 40508 in a user database session and appears to work only on a master connection, which is a provisioning session. Creating an application identity is inverted from the cloud: a SQL contained user fails with Msg 15007 and turning containment on fails with Msg 12844, so the answer is a server login with a mapped database user, which is the opposite of the guidance for Azure SQL Database. BULK INSERT from a local file fails with Msg 12713, because the source has to be Azure Blob Storage. And the current gap list is a live document, so the honest answer names the live known limitations page rather than reciting a snapshot that will age.",
  "posture": ["read", "inspect"],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": ["azure-sql-db-container"],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-container",
    "azuresql-db-auth",
    "azuresql-db-local-to-cloud",
    "azuresql-db-import",
    "azuresql-db-feedback"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "setup": [
      "the Azure SQL Database container running, with a provisioned user database"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "each answer names which of the four buckets the feature falls into",
      "no answer claims a managed service feature exists on the container",
      "no answer calls a non-x64 host supported; it runs under emulation",
      "any answer about a current gap links the live known limitations page rather than a snapshot"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": { "rows": [[5, "SQL Azure"]] },
        "why": "The first bucket in the skill's mental model, made executable: this is the Azure SQL Database engine and not the SQL Server image, which is the premise every other answer in the skill rests on. If this returns anything else, the four bucket sort is being applied to the wrong product."
      },
      {
        "sql": "BACKUP DATABASE master TO DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_backup.bak';",
        "expect": { "error": 40510 },
        "why": "The most asked question in the skill, and one it answers with a specific number in three places: SKILL.md, references/faq.md and references/limitations.md all say Msg 40510. The database named is master rather than the scratch database because the statement filter refuses the statement before it resolves the name, and the skill's own claim is that this fails in every session."
      },
      {
        "sql": "RESTORE DATABASE master FROM DISK = N'/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_backup.bak';",
        "expect": { "error": 40510 },
        "why": "The skill claims the same number for RESTORE as for BACKUP, and the two are worth separating because the answer people act on is the restore half: it is what somebody reaches for after losing a container. If RESTORE were merely failing because the file does not exist, this probe would report a file error instead, which is itself the signal that the statement is not being filtered."
      },
      {
        "sql": "USE master;",
        "expect": { "error": 40508 },
        "why": "The second most asked question, and the one whose answer is easiest to get half right. The refusal is a property of the session, not of the engine: this probe runs in the scratch user database and must fail, whereas the same statement on a master connection succeeds because master is a provisioning session. A pass here also confirms the runner is not accidentally connected to master, which would quietly invalidate the two probes above."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER probe_faq_contained_user WITH PASSWORD = 'Pr0be_Passw0rd!x';",
        "expect": { "error": 15007 },
        "why": "The answer here is inverted from Azure SQL Database, which is what makes it worth a probe: in the cloud a contained user is the recommended shape, and on the container it fails, so the correct guidance is a server login plus a mapped user. Msg 15007 is stated in this skill's SKILL.md and again in references/faq.md, and was measured on the container rather than taken from documentation. The password in this probe is a throwaway for a user that never exists."
      },
      {
        "sql": "ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CONTAINMENT = PARTIAL;",
        "expect": { "error": 12844 },
        "why": "The obvious workaround for the refusal above, and it is also refused, which is why the skill's answer sends people to a login and a mapped user rather than to a database setting. The engine says Msg 12844, 'ALTER DATABASE statement failed; this functionality is not available in the current edition of SQL Server.' Measured on build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure'. This skill's SKILL.md and references/faq.md previously carried a different, unmeasured error number and were corrected on the same integration branch as this probe, so body and probe now agree. Without this probe the previous one invites the reader to try the containment setting and conclude the skill was simply missing a step."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE TABLE dbo.probe_faq_bulk (col1 nvarchar(100) NULL);\nBULK INSERT dbo.probe_faq_bulk FROM '/var/opt/mssql/data/probe_faq_nonexistent.csv' WITH (FIELDTERMINATOR = ',', ROWTERMINATOR = '\\n');",
        "expect": { "error": 12713 },
        "why": "A local file is not a valid source: the engine reads from Azure Blob Storage, so the loading answer for a laptop CSV is a client side tool and not BULK INSERT. Msg 12713 is stated in this skill's references/faq.md. The file deliberately does not exist, which is the point: the statement is refused for being local before anything tries to open it, so a file-not-found error here would mean the restriction has been lifted and the skill's answer needs rewriting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the azuresql-db-faq skill",
      "What can the Azure SQL Database container do and not do",
      "Is this feature supported on the Azure SQL Database container",
      "How does the container differ from Azure SQL Database in the cloud"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "can I take a backup of this container",
      "why does USE fail here when it works on my other SQL container",
      "why do I have to create the database before I can connect to it",
      "is there an ARM64 build of this image",
      "why can I not find the image on Docker Hub",
      "why does my create user statement fail when I add an application account",
      "my engine seems to be missing a fix that shipped last week",
      "my query works locally and then fails in Azure, why",
      "can I run this container in production"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "explain why the local engine refuses something my SQL Server does happily",
      "tell me which limitations are permanent and which are preview gaps"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Set up a local PostgreSQL database with docker compose",
      "Provision an Azure SQL Database in my Azure subscription",
      "Connect to SQL Server on my company network",
      "Start the Azure SQL Database container and check it is ready",
      "Report a bug in the container",
      "Rewrite my docker-compose from the SQL Server image to the Azure SQL Database container",
      "Create an application login and grant it the right roles",
      "Store embeddings and run a similarity search locally",
      "Load this CSV into my local database"
    ]
  }
}
